---
import Center from "../../components/Center.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="How to Generate Google Slides From a Template">
  <Center>
    <div class="my-8">
      <div class="mb-1">
        <a href="/help" class="hover:underline">← Help</a>
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-8">
        How to Generate Google Slides From a Template
      </h1>

      <figure class="mb-8">
        <iframe
          class="w-full aspect-video h-auto border border-gray-300 rounded-lg shadow-lg"
          width="560"
          height="315"
          src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/Rd0wdIux4xw?si=WR_1P8JLW1AoFqni"
          title="YouTube video player"
          frameborder="0"
          allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share"
          referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
          allowfullscreen></iframe>
        <figcaption class="text-center text-lg text-gray-700 mt-4 italic">
          Short demonstration video. Click to play!
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div
        class="prose prose-code:bg-gray-100 prose-code:border prose-code:border-gray-300 prose-code:px-1 prose-code:py-px prose-code:rounded prose-code:before:content-none prose-code:after:content-none"
      >
        <h1 id="step-1-install-slide-stencil">Step 1: Install Slide Stencil</h1>
        <p>
          <a href="https://www.slidestencil.com/">Slide Stencil</a> is the Google
          Slides Add-on I built that helps you generate Google Slides from a template.
          To install it, open <a
            href="https://workspace.google.com/marketplace/app/slide_stencil_by_mr_pullen/8639039219"
            >Slide Stencil in the Google Add-on Marketplace</a
          > and click "Install".
        </p>
        <h1 id="step-2-open-slide-stencil-in-google-slides">
          Step 2: Open Slide Stencil in Google Slides
        </h1>
        <p>
          Next, you'll need to open the Slide Stencil add-on in Google Slides.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>Open your slideshow</li>
          <li>
            At the top, click <code
              >Extensions &gt; Slide Stencil by Mr. Pullen &gt; Open</code
            >
          </li>
          <li>
            If requested, grant permission for the add-on to access your current
            slideshow
          </li>
        </ol>
        <h1 id="step-3-design-your-template-slide">
          Step 3: Design your template slide
        </h1>
        <p>
          Your template slide is the one that Slide Stencil will generate new
          versions of. A template slide is a regular slide, but it contains <strong
            >variables</strong
          >. A variable is a slot where different content will be inserted for
          each generated slide.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a
            href="https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/1Fx62M08AUU2krx7TC0YIE0GeqQapdAZkW1TMsUiHu3o/edit?slide=id.g3d92bf5af49_1_102#slide=id.g3d92bf5af49_1_102"
            >See the example templates ↗</a
          >
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong
            >To create a variable, just type double curly braces inside any text
            box or shape.</strong
          > For example, you can type <code>{`{{ date }}`}</code> or <code
            >{`{{ title }}`}</code
          > anywhere on your slide and those will become the variables "date" or "title".
        </p>
        <p>
          There's nothing special about those names, either! <strong
            >You can name your variable anything you want.</strong
          > Whatever words you type inside the curly braces will become your column
          name in the next step.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you want to insert unique <strong>images</strong> in each generated slide
          (not just text), <a
            href="/help/how-to-generate-google-slides-with-images-from-a-template"
            >see this tutorial</a
          >.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong
            >When you're done designing your template slide, click "Select
            Template Slide" in the Slide Stencil sidebar on the right.</strong
          >
        </p>
        <h1 id="step-4-prepare-your-template-data">
          Step 4: Prepare your template data
        </h1>
        <p>
          Now that you have your template slide prepared, we can start to think
          about generating new versions of the template.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong
            >In Slide Stencil, you'll put your data in a table. Each row of the
            table will become one slide.</strong
          >
        </p>
        <p>
          After completing step 3 and clicking the "Select Template Slide"
          button, Slide Stencil will give you an editable table where you can
          type your data. Each template variable will become one column. Each
          row of the table will turn into a slide.
        </p>
        <p>Enter your data into the table.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            If you have any image variables, you'll need to put the image URL in
            the cell. <a
              href="/help/how-to-generate-google-slides-with-images-from-a-template"
              >Learn more here.</a
            >
          </li>
          <li>
            If you want to save your data to reuse in the future, you might want
            to connect a Google Sheet.
          </li>
          <li>
            If you want to use formulas, keyboard shortcuts, or other power-user
            features, you might want to connect a Google Sheet.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <h1 id="step-5-generate-slides">Step 5: Generate Slides</h1>
        <p>
          This is the fun part! Once you've completed steps 1-4, all you need to
          do is click the "Generate Slides" button. Slide Stencil will create
          all your slides for you automatically.
        </p>
        <p>Generation might take a few seconds, so please be patient.</p>
        <p>
          If you want to make changes to your template after-the-fact, you'll
          have to delete the generated slides, edit the template, and then
          re-generate.
        </p>
        <h1 id="i-make-more-apps-to-help-teachers-">
          I make more apps to help teachers!
        </h1>
        <p>
          If you like Shuffle Buddy, you might like <a
            href="https://www.joshuapullen.com/">the other apps I've made</a
          >. I am always building things to help teachers.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <a href="https://www.shufflebuddy.com/">Shuffle Buddy</a> for classroom
            seating charts
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://www.rocketspelling.com/">Rocket Spelling</a> for elementary
            spelling practice
          </li>
          <li>...And <a href="https://www.joshuapullen.com/">many more</a>!</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Best, Josh</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Center>
</Layout>
